"You are the independent adversarial reviewer for federation-recon PR #174 / issue #173.

This is a HIGH-risk review of a new trust-boundary control. Work only in the disposable detached checkout supplied as your current directory. Do not commit, push, merge, contact GitHub, access credentials, invoke any external model/API, or modify any repository outside this checkout. You may make temporary local mutations in this disposable checkout and run bounded targeted tests. Do not run the full gate; it is slow and the operator runs it separately. Do not invoke scripts/consult-opencode.sh against a live provider from inside this review.

Read, in this order:

1. CLAUDE.md
2. docs/operator-lessons.md
3. governance/adversarial-review.md
4. governance/reviewers.md
5. operator/work-orders/173.json

Then inspect every changed byte with:

git diff main...HEAD -- . ':(exclude)governance/consultations/*.diff'

Also read the unchanged substrate used by the change, especially scripts/lib/consultation-provenance.sh, scripts/gate.sh, the relevant test manifest, and the OpenCode CLI assumptions exercised by scripts/test/opencode-consult.bats.

Apply all questions 1 through 11 from governance/adversarial-review.md. Execute the cheapest meaningful evasions and mutations you can perform without network access, and report exact commands/results and whether each idea was executed or only reasoned about. Pay special attention to:

- whether a plausible but mismatched, ambiguous, partial, crashed, or timed-out response can become a consultation;
- whether stream/session/export binding can be defeated by valid JSON shapes the tests omit;
- process-group cleanup and leftover worktrees;
- unsafe output paths, dirty-source handling, and concurrent or changed files;
- whether the emitted Markdown really passes the existing consultation provenance gate for the right reason;
- what the OpenCode export proves about the service provider versus what it cannot prove about the upstream model provider;
- whether documentation overclaims independence, sandboxing, or provider identity;
- whether any existing protection was weakened;
- whether the test oracle merely duplicates the implementation.

Claims from the draft PR that you must verify rather than trust:

- 11 adapter tests and 37 consultation-provenance tests pass;
- wrong-provider and missing-provenance mutations turn targeted tests red;
- the adapter accepts only one normally completed stream session whose exported service provider/model match the request;
- failed output is quarantined rather than published as a consultation;
- the disposable worktree isolates repository edits but is not an OS sandbox;
- the control establishes OpenCode Go service routing, not the upstream company behind the model label.

The operator's separate full-gate result may not yet be available in your checkout; label it unverified rather than inferring it. This is the first review round. Be blunt, reject if any blocking condition remains, and do not return APPROVE with conditions.

End the response with exactly one final line:

verdict: APPROVE

or

verdict: REJECT"

